{
  "acceptance": [
    "The comment scanner identifies and decomposes command strings passed to recognized runners like ssh, watch, or xargs into individual commands for interpreter scanning",
    "Regression test verifies comments in single-string runner invocations are detected",
    "The whitespace check at scripts/comment_execution.py:655 still detects a nested interpreter whose path contains a space, for example `/opt/My Tools/python3 -c` under docker exec (oracle finding D4; today's develop detects it). A regression test covers it.",
    "The cost of the broader shell gate regex is measured on the same one-commit transition run used in comment-policy-hook-saturates-shared-host (358 s wall, 276 s user CPU baseline) and reported; the change does not make that run slower."
  ],
  "context": "Implemented in worktree branch feat/comment-scanner-enhancements: runner decomposition for ssh/watch/xargs/env/sudo/nohup, nested command scanning, and preserved Python runner detection for executable paths containing spaces. Prior PR #2821 D1-D9 changes remain in the same PR.\n\n2026-10-10 review fixes: plain `sudo python3 -c`, `env python3 -c`, and `nohup python3 -c` already yielded findings. `sudo -u nobody ...` returned a coverage error and `sudo --unknown ...` returned clean; sudo/nohup now use registered option parsing, while env retains strict `command_words` normalization. `printf %%` now emits one `%`; pipeline extraction handles the first producer/consumer pair and fails closed on a dynamic immediate consumer. Regression tests cover these cases.\n\nFinal one-commit head is 91d3d419c07592d17f2ea712a065153027efa769, exactly one commit after f4eed2d403ec53aa254b96e249c52bf232b09038; worktree clean. `make pr-preflight` passed 739 tests; focused D10-D12 regressions passed 17 cases, including unspaced dynamic consumer and quoted sed delimiter controls. Workflow expression tests passed 89 cases. Isolated npm install from the CI manifest/lock passed with npm 11.19.1; the MDX scanner test passed four cases with the isolated TypeScript path. Commit hook passed.\n\nPerformance evidence: the first final pre-optimization paired A/B showed old-regex 281.69s wall / 225.96s user / 49.13s sys vs candidate 288.08s / 231.49s / 49.99s; both passed 5,776 files with zero violations. Regex was tightened to horizontal whitespace and a shorter dynamic prefix. On the optimized head, baseline regex passed at 409.81s / 315.37s / 49.08s / 478,461,952 bytes max RSS; optimized candidate passed at 295.88s / 237.97s / 50.51s / 575,832,064 bytes. The 28% wall variance across adjacent runs makes this A/B inconclusive for regex cost. Both runs scanned 5,776 files with zero violations/enforced failures. Historical tracker baseline: 358s wall / 276s user / 44s sys / 476MB RSS. Run one repeat baseline/candidate pair sequentially on the exact final head to assess the delta under comparable load; report variance honestly and confirm no consistent slowdown.\n\nAfter benchmark acceptance, update PR #2821, reply to and resolve D5-D12 with evidence, obtain current-head Oracle SHIP and green required CI, arm auto-merge, and monitor through merge. Keep active until PR merges.",
  "description": "Commands passed to execution runners such as ssh, watch or xargs as a single quoted string argument were not tokenized or decomposed into subcommands. As a result, nested interpreter invocations (e.g. ssh host python -c '...') inside runner strings could bypass comment and docstring scanning. The work lives in scripts/comment_payloads.py (the RUNNER_COMMANDS table and the shell gate regex) and scripts/comment_execution.py (the Python-side runner string handling). Extend the analyzer to split runner arguments and inspect nested scripts.\n\nPR #2821 implements this but has an oracle regression (D4, below) that must be fixed before merge.",
